A||||9||SpeedPhreak||any one into or know about vw bugs?||||1||
Z||000000||SpeedPhreak||09-27-2000||01:49 PM||||i found another sterling.. but this one is built on a bug (as opposed to a custom chassis).. its a 62 pan.. doesnt that have the link pin suspension?<BR>does that suspension have performance potential? if not is it easily upgraded to the ball joint suspension.. or are there performance after market suspensions available?<P>everything else is pretty much redone.. a new 1835 engine new trans & clutch so the suspension is what im most worried about.<P>thanx.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||09-27-2000||08:48 PM||||I think that it does have the link pin suspension and the swing arm on the rear. If it is a stock VW pan, why not just swap in a later model with IRS and ball joints?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-27-2000||09:12 PM||||Yeah, It should be the old suspension and single jointed rear shafts.<P>I don't think they started A arm front untill near the end of them in the 70's.<P>The double jointed rear shafts started in the late 60's I think. All the old beattles I ever saw had the swing arms in rear but the double jointed drive shafts did away with the camber problems. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||09-28-2000||12:17 AM||||I have worked on may bugs and done many suspension mods. Yes a 62 has a link front end and a swing axle rear. What I found to work best for me was either converting the rear to IRS or swapping to a late model pan. And keeping the link front end. But not to late. You will get into the strut front ends which in my opinion are not that great.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever2||09-28-2000||02:28 AM||||I was into VW's, sand buggies and Baja Bugs.  Lots of those people avoid the Super Beetle (later model) because of the ball joint/strut front end.  The rear end won't matter much but IRS is better.  Judge the car on its other qualities, you can change suspension later if desired.  What you described should be fine.  Have you asked Mike McBride of Solid Sterling?  He has always written back when I had questions... PO Box 927, St Helens OR  97051<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cheever2||09-28-2000||02:43 AM||||I knew a guy that had a Sterling with a VW engine.  He drove it over 100 mph from Broken Arrow to Tulsa (Oklahoma of course) then had to slow down to 20 or so.  Being air-cooled, the back end got REAL hot and started fire to the closest part of the body.  With that canopy, he barely got out of it.  Showed me a newspaper clipping w/ photo and there wasn't much left.  Dude bought it turnkey for 15 g's.  I remember that every time I think about getting one.<BR>||||reg||1
